/Title (Modern symmetric multiprocessing \(SMP\) architectures maintain hardware cache coherency via snooping protocols such as MESI \(Modified, Exclusive, Shared, Invalid\). Data moves between main memory \(DRAM\) and CPU caches in discrete 64-byte blocks called cache lines. False Sharing occurs when two independent threads executing on physically separate CPU cores continuously write to distinct variables that happen to reside on the same 64-byte cache line. Although no logical data race exists, each write forces the core's cache controller to broadcast an Invalidation signal across the interconnect bus, evicting the line from the other core's L1/L2 cache and causing massive pipeline stalls.)
/Dest [12 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1351 0 R
/Prev 1407 0 R
/Next 1408 0 R
>>
endobj
1418 0 obj
<<
/Title (Create 'task2_falsesharing.py'. Run the benchmark 3 separate times to eliminate background OS scheduling noise. The test compares 4 threads writing to adjacent list indices vs. 4 threads writing to padded indices separated by 16 elements \(16 * 8 bytes = 128 bytes, guaranteeing placement on distinct 64-byte cache lines\). Record the median execution times.)
